 \begin{document}
 \maketitle
 \begin{abstract}
-A saddle and a numerical descent do not by themselves establish a globally optimal escape route. We certify the annihilation barrier of a neutral dipole in the exact-cosine XY model on the infinite square lattice. Admissible paths are strongly continuous in the homogeneous energy space and end at any physical uniform phase lift. A finite atlas, together with rigorous control of its infinite-dimensional transverse fibers, separates a local minimum from every endpoint. Exact-saddle caps and a validated upper path identify the barrier with an asymmetric index-one saddle height. In unit-coupling normalization, the barrier lies strictly between 0.00567867 and 0.00570116. We formulate reusable a posteriori tests for signed-bond coercivity, full dual residuals, and complete boundary coverage. An explicit lattice Green projection estimate gives inverse-radius tails for the critical states and the certified path. We also prove quantitative robustness under arbitrary summable coupling perturbations of total norm at most 0.002. The minimum and two distinct index-one saddles persist, while the global barrier remains positive and changes by at most twice the perturbation norm. The continued saddles are not yet identified as perturbed minimax realizers. The result combines a lower bound over the full admissible path class with a matching upper construction, effective exterior estimates, and a uniform perturbation theorem. Frozen sources and a fresh execution record make the computational certificate reproducible.
+A saddle and a numerical descent do not by themselves establish a globally optimal escape route. We certify the annihilation barrier of a neutral dipole at separation twelve in the exact-cosine XY model on the infinite square lattice. Admissible paths are strongly continuous in the homogeneous energy space and end at any physical uniform phase lift. A finite atlas, together with rigorous control of its infinite-dimensional transverse fibers, separates a local minimum from every endpoint. Exact-saddle caps and a validated upper path identify the barrier as the energy difference between an asymmetric index-one saddle and the local minimum. In unit-coupling normalization, the barrier lies strictly between 0.00567867 and 0.00570116. We formulate reusable a posteriori tests for signed-bond coercivity, full dual residuals, and complete boundary coverage. An explicit lattice Green projection estimate gives inverse-radius tails for the critical states and the certified path. We also prove quantitative robustness under arbitrary summable coupling perturbations of total norm at most 0.002. The minimum and two distinct index-one saddles persist, while the global barrier remains positive and changes by at most twice the perturbation norm. The continued saddles are not yet identified as perturbed minimax realizers. The result combines a lower bound over the full admissible path class with a matching upper construction, effective exterior estimates, and a uniform perturbation theorem. Frozen sources and a fresh execution record document the computational certificate.
 \end{abstract}
 \begin{keywords}
 XY lattice, annihilation barrier, computer-assisted proof, index-one saddle, a posteriori verification, lattice Green function
